## Summary

I shipped a production-polished, native Apple (SwiftUI) parity port of `AIFeedbackQueueTriage` — the Helix feedback-queue triage advisor — at parity with `web/src/components/ai/AIFeedbackQueueTriage.tsx`, following the established `AIRangePrediction` (0043) reference pattern.

**Delivered (10 files, 2183 lines, committed `c888d0bb1`):**
- `.Adapter` (request/SSE-parse/stream-reducer/output+action derivations), `.Model` (P1/S8 state-holder, P1/S10 i18n facade, P1/S11 telemetry seam), `.Projection` (pure view-state), `.Views` + `.Chrome` (HIG-native SwiftUI using P1/S9 tokens), `.swift` (the View), `.Previews`, `.strings` (i18n catalog), and 2 test files.

**Key parity decisions:**
- `canStart`/`haveFeedback` requires `feedbackId > 0` (nil/0/negative disabled) — unlike the vehicle-id surfaces where 0 is valid; body ships `{feedback_id: haveFeedback ? id : 0}` to `/ai/feedback/triage/draft`.
- **Propose-only** contract preserved: the web source references `useUpdateFeedback` only in comments to state the advisor never mutates — so I bind only the stream + i18n (no mutation peer).
- All states reproduced (gated/loading/error/ready × empty/no-feedback/thinking/prose/stream-error × live/stale/offline) + VoiceOver/Dynamic Type/Reduce Motion.

**Verification (all green for my surface):**
- Isolated SwiftPM XCTest against the **real** design system (Swift 6, macOS 15): **74/74 pass**.
- swiftlint --strict **0** · swiftformat --lint **0/9** · placeholder gate **0** · i18n **4/4** web keys (description byte-exact, 368 chars).
- Real `xcodebuild` iOS + macOS: my 7 sources compiled clean, **0 errors / 0 warnings mine**, `TeslaSync` module emitted (no type collisions).

